F1=('<!--noart-->'+FR_CSS
    +'<div class="fr-split" data-part="split"><p><b>Free radicals.</b> Oxygen free radicals from normal metabolism, and the damage they leave. Harman, Journal of Gerontology, 1956. The wording used here is Pérez and colleagues, 2009, citing that paper.</p>'
    '<p><b>Rate of living.</b> Life span inverse to the average mass-specific metabolic rate. Pearl, 1928, as stated by Vaanholt and colleagues, 2009. Not a count of radicals.</p>'
    '<p><b>A mitochondrial clock.</b> Harman, 1972, as described by Sastre, Pallardó, and Viña, 2000. Miquel and colleagues, 1980, and Miquel, 1992, put the mitochondrial genome in the same argument.</p></div>'
    +f'<p class="fr-quote" data-part="verbatim">{fr_esc(FR_VERBATIM)}</p>'
    +'<ol class="fr-tl" style="margin-top:12px">'
    '<li data-part="pearl"><span class="yr">1928</span><div><span class="fr-k">Rate of living</span><b>Pearl, The Rate of Living.</b> No DOI in Crossref. Vaanholt and colleagues, 2009: life span and average mass-specific metabolic rate are inversely proportional. They say that version was later rejected. Birds have higher metabolic rates than mammals of the same size and yet live longer.</div></li>'
    '<li data-part="harman1956"><span class="yr">1956</span><div><span class="fr-k">Free radicals</span><b>Harman, Journal of Gerontology.</b> No abstract in Europe PMC. Pérez and colleagues, citing it: oxygen free radicals formed in normal metabolism play a role in aging because oxidative damage to macromolecules increases. Harman, PNAS, 1981: the theory offers an account of why mammalian life span tracks basal metabolic rate. That 1981 sentence is the link to Pearl. It is not a line read out of the 1956 paper.</div></li>'
    '<li data-part="harman1972"><span class="yr">1972</span><div><span class="fr-k">Mitochondria</span><b>Harman, Journal of the American Geriatrics Society.</b> The Biologic Clock: The Mitochondria? No abstract in Europe PMC. Sastre, Pallardó, and Viña, 2000: he suggested mitochondria might be the biological clock, the rate of oxygen consumption setting the rate at which free-radical reactions damage mitochondria. The title is a question.</div></li>'
    f'<li data-part="miquel"><span class="yr">1980</span><div><span class="fr-k">Mitochondrial DNA</span><b>Miquel, Economos, Fleming, and Johnson, Experimental Gerontology.</b> Mitochondrial role in cell aging. No abstract in Europe PMC. Miquel, Mutation Research, 1992, states the early-1980s hypothesis: aging may be caused by mutation, inactivation, or loss of mitochondrial DNA in irreversibly differentiated cells. He writes that this genome carries the structural genes for {FR["miquelProteins"]} hydrophobic proteins of the respiratory chain and ATP synthase. The claim that it lacks excision and recombination repair is his argument in that paper.</div></li>'
    '<li data-part="sohal"><span class="yr">1996</span><div><span class="fr-k">Oxidative stress</span><b>Sohal and Weindruch, Science.</b> Damage increases with age and is postulated to be a major cause of senescence. Three supports, with no new numbers attached here: antioxidant enzymes extend maximum life span in transgenic Drosophila; species longevity runs inversely with mitochondrial superoxide and hydrogen peroxide; caloric restriction lowers oxidative damage and extends maximum life span in mammals. The species claim is about the radical rate, not Pearl\'s metabolic rate.</div></li>'
    +'</ol>'+fr_src([_S_PE,_S_VA,_S_H56,_S_H81,_S_PEZ,_S_H72,_S_SA,_S_MI80,_S_MI92,_S_SW]))
F2=('<!--noart-->'
    +'<p class="fr-note" data-part="scheme">A path, not a measurement. The boxes are not amounts, and no fraction of electron flow is printed. This lesson does not have a source in front of it for a leak percentage, so it does not draw one. Superoxide and hydrogen peroxide are the two species Sohal and Weindruch name.</p>'
    +'<div class="fr-flow">'
    +'<div class="fr-box" data-part="chain"><b>Where it is made.</b> Mitochondria consume the oxygen, in the account Sastre and colleagues give of the 1972 suggestion. The respiratory chain is that consumption, used to make ATP. Some of the chemistry leaves superoxide. Miquel, 1992, puts mitochondrial DNA at the inner membrane, near that chemistry. Production, the genome, and the damage stay three different objects on this figure.</div>'
    +'<p class="fr-arr">Superoxide dismutase</p>'
    +'<div class="fr-box" data-part="clear"><b>What clears it.</b> Superoxide dismutase makes hydrogen peroxide. Manganese superoxide dismutase is the mitochondrial enzyme cut to half in the Sod2 mice on the next figure. Catalase and glutathione peroxidase dispose of hydrogen peroxide. Schriner and colleagues put extra human catalase in the peroxisome, the nucleus, or the mitochondria. The months in the abstract belong to the mitochondrial line. This box does not invent a dose.</div>'
    +'<p class="fr-arr">If it is not cleared</p>'
    +f'<div class="fr-box" data-part="damage"><b>What the damage is.</b> Oxidation of lipids, proteins, and DNA, nuclear and mitochondrial. The markers in the papers here are an oxidized deoxyguanosine in DNA, malondialdehyde and isoprostanes in lipids, and protein carbonyls. A higher marker means more of that damage was measured. It does not, by itself, say the damage caused the aging.</div>'
    +f'<div class="fr-box" data-part="age"><b>Does it accumulate?</b> Mecocci and colleagues, 1993: {_Mec["n"]} normal humans, ages {_Mec["ageLow"]} to {_Mec["ageHigh"]}, cortex and cerebellum. The oxidized nucleoside rose with age in nuclear DNA and faster in mitochondrial DNA. Mitochondrial DNA had {_Mec["mtVsNuclearWhole"]}-fold more than nuclear DNA in the whole set, and {_Mec["mtVsNuclearOver70"]}-fold more in those older than {_Mec["overAge"]}. Those folds are mitochondrial versus nuclear, not old versus young. No fold for the rise with age is in the abstract. Hamilton and colleagues, 2001, isolated DNA with sodium iodide to limit oxidation during the prep, and still found a significant rise with age in nuclear DNA of every tissue and rodent strain they studied, and in liver mitochondrial DNA. Dietary restriction reduced that accumulation. No fold is in the abstract.</div>'
    +'</div>'+fr_src([_S_SW,_S_SA,_S_MI92,_S_SC,_S_ME,_S_HA,_S_VR]))
_jama_rows=[{'name':'All trials together','rr':_J['allRr'],'ci':_J['allCi']},
            {'name':'Low-bias trials','rr':_J['lowBiasRr'],'ci':_J['lowBiasCi']}]+_J['rows']
F3=('<!--noart-->'
    +'<p class="fr-note" data-part="how">Each row is a prediction, then the result the named paper reported. Nothing here was recalculated. Where an abstract prints no sample size, no percentage, or no lifespan in days, none is added.</p>'
    +'<div class="fr-rows">'
    +f'<div class="fr-row" data-part="agepred"><span class="tag">Accumulation · supported as a correlation</span><p><b>Prediction.</b> Oxidative damage increases with age. <b>Result.</b> Mecocci, Hamilton, and Van Remmen, as on the previous figure, found increases in the markers and tissues they measured. Sohal and Weindruch had already listed the increase as support. The cause is not settled by a correlation with age.</p></div>'
    +f'<div class="fr-row" data-part="mcat"><span class="tag">Mitochondrial catalase · supported in that mouse</span><p><b>Prediction.</b> Less hydrogen peroxide inside mitochondria lengthens life. <b>Result.</b> Schriner and colleagues, 2005. Human catalase in the peroxisome, the nucleus, or the mitochondria. Median and maximum lifespans were maximally increased in the mitochondria-targeted animals: averages of {_Sc["medianMonths"]} months and {_Sc["maximumMonths"]} months. Cardiac pathology and cataract were delayed, oxidative damage fell, hydrogen peroxide production and hydrogen-peroxide inactivation of aconitase were attenuated, and mitochondrial deletions developed less. The abstract does not print the other two lifespans, and it does not give the median they started from, so {_Sc["medianMonths"]} months is not turned into a percentage here.</p></div>'
    +f'<div class="fr-row" data-part="mutator"><span class="tag">Mitochondrial mutations · the phenotype is real, the oxidative step is not</span><p><b>Prediction.</b> Mitochondrial DNA mutations should age a mouse early, and, if free radicals are the path, oxidative damage should rise with them. <b>Result.</b> Trifunovic and colleagues, 2004. Proofreading-deficient PolgA. A {_fold3} rise in point mutations, more deleted mitochondrial DNA, a reduced lifespan, and early weight loss, less subcutaneous fat, hair loss, spine curvature, osteoporosis, anaemia, reduced fertility, and heart enlargement. Kujoth and colleagues, 2005, the same kind of polymerase: accelerated-aging features, not associated with increased markers of oxidative stress, correlated with apoptosis instead, especially in tissues that turn over quickly. Do not paste Kujoth\'s oxidative-stress result onto Trifunovic. Trifunovic\'s abstract does not report it.</p></div>'
    +f'<div class="fr-row" data-part="species"><span class="tag">Species · direction yes, phylogeny-controlled regression no</span><p><b>Prediction.</b> Longer-lived species produce less mitochondrial reactive oxygen species. <b>Result.</b> Lambert and colleagues, 2007, hydrogen peroxide from isolated heart mitochondria. The abstract\'s species are {_spp}. Rates during succinate oxidation were generally lower in the longer-lived species, at complex I during reverse electron transport. Maximum lifespan correlated negatively with that production, and more so after body mass was removed. After phylogeny was removed, the inverse association was significant by a sign test and fell short of significance by regression. No correlation coefficient is in the abstract.</p></div>'
    +f'<div class="fr-row" data-part="mole"><span class="tag">Naked mole-rat · not the prediction</span><p><b>Prediction.</b> A very long life should come with low oxidative damage, or with strong antioxidants. <b>Result.</b> Andziak and colleagues, Aging Cell, 2006: maximum lifespan greater than {_Ad["mlsMoreThan"]} years, an order of magnitude longer than body size predicts. Against similar-sized mice: {_Ad["lipidPeroxidationFold"]}-fold higher in vivo lipid peroxidation, accrued lipid damage {_Ad["lipidAccrued"]}, DNA about {_Ad["dna"]}, proteins {_Ad["protein"]}, versus physiologically age-matched mice, and equal to same-aged mice. Andziak and Buffenstein, the same year: mouse maximum lifespan about {_Al["mouseMlsAbout"]} years. Young mole-rats were already at least {_Al["youngAtLeast"]} higher in lipid peroxidation and iron, P &lt; {_Al["pYoung"]}. Old mice had {_Al["oldMice"]} the accrued damage of young mice. Mole-rat urinary isoprostanes fell {_Al["isoprostane"]} with age, P &lt; {_Al["pIsoprostane"]}, and lipid damage did not change with age. Andziak, O\'Connor, and Buffenstein, 2005: survival greater than {_Ag["mlsMoreThanYears"]} years, eight times the similar-sized mouse. Liver cellular glutathione peroxidase {_Ag["gpxTimesLower"]} times lower, p &lt; {_Ag["p"]}, mole-rats at {_nmr_m} months against mice at {_mou_m} months. Lambert\'s list includes the naked mole-rat, and that paper measured hydrogen peroxide production, not this tissue damage.</p></div>'
    +f'<div class="fr-row" data-part="genes"><span class="tag">Antioxidant genes · the simple prediction fails</span><p><b>Prediction.</b> Less superoxide dismutase shortens life. <b>Result.</b> Van Remmen and colleagues, 2003. Sod2 heterozygotes, {_Vr["mnSodPercent"]} percent of manganese superoxide dismutase in every tissue, for life. At {_Vr["ageMonths"]} months, nuclear 8-oxodG was {_Vr["heartPercent"]} percent higher in heart to over {_Vr["liverOverPercent"]} percent higher in liver. Mitochondrial DNA from liver and brain was also higher. The number of old mice with a tumor was {_Vr["tumorPercent"]} percent higher. Mean and maximum survival were identical. Pérez and colleagues, 2009: of {_Pz["manipulations"]} genetic manipulations in their laboratory, only Sod1 deletion affected lifespan, about a {_Pz["sod1ShorterPercent"]} percent shorter mean, median, and maximum. They say it is uncertain whether that is accelerated aging or novel pathology, including hepatocellular carcinoma never observed in their C57BL/6 account. Van Raamsdonk and Hekimi, 2009: none of the {_W["sodGenes"]} worm sod deletions shortened life. sod-2 deletion extended it. The abstract does not print how many days that single mutant gained. Deleting sod-2 in clk-1 worms increased mean lifespan by {_W["clk1MeanDays"]} days. Deleting it in isp-1 worms decreased lifespan by {_W["isp1Days"]} days. Those days are the double mutants. Van Raamsdonk and Hekimi, 2012: worms with no superoxide dismutase left, sod-12345, are viable and have a normal lifespan.</p></div>'
    +f'<div class="fr-row" data-part="pills"><span class="tag">Antioxidant supplements · no benefit, and harm in the low-bias trials</span><p><b>Prediction.</b> Antioxidant pills lower mortality. <b>JAMA, 2007.</b> {_J["trials"]} trials, {fr_n(_J["participants"])} participants, searched to October 2005. All trials together, relative risk {_J["allRr"]}, interval {fr_ci(_J["allCi"])}, not significant. {_J["lowBiasTrials"]} low-bias trials, {fr_n(_J["lowBiasParticipants"])} participants, relative risk {_J["lowBiasRr"]}, interval {fr_ci(_J["lowBiasCi"])}. Beta-carotene, vitamin A, and vitamin E, after selenium trials were excluded, are the three rows. Vitamin C and selenium had no significant effect in that abstract, and it prints no relative risk for that vitamin C sentence.</p>'
    +fr_rr_table(_jama_rows)
    +f'<p><b>Cochrane, 2008, then the 2012 update.</b> The 2008 review is not a 2007 paper. {_C8["trials"]} trials, {fr_n(_C8["participants"])} participants. Low-bias relative risk {_C8["lowBiasRr"]}, interval {fr_ci(_C8["lowBiasCi"])}. Close to the JAMA tally, and not the same tally. The 2012 update searched to February 2011. {_C2["trials"]} trials, {fr_n(_C2["participants"])} participants. {_C2["lowBiasTrials"]} low-bias trials, {fr_n(_C2["lowBiasParticipants"])} participants. Mean age {_C2["meanAge"]}, range {_C2["ageLow"]} to {_C2["ageHigh"]}. Random-effects relative risk {_C2["randomRr"]}, interval {fr_ci(_C2["randomCi"])}, not significant. Fixed-effect {_C2["fixedRr"]}, interval {fr_ci(_C2["fixedCi"])}. I-squared {_C2["i2"]} percent. Low-bias relative risk {_C2["lowBiasRr"]}, interval {fr_ci(_C2["lowBiasCi"])}. The authors found no evidence to support the supplements. Beta-carotene and vitamin E seemed to increase mortality, and so may higher doses of vitamin A. The dose meta-regression for vitamin A was relative risk {_C2["vitaminADoseRr"]}, interval {fr_ci(_C2["vitaminADoseCi"])}, P = {_C2["vitaminADoseP"]}.</p>'
    +fr_rr_table(_C2['rows'], trial_col=True)
    +'</div></div>'+fr_src([_S_ME,_S_HA,_S_SW,_S_SC,_S_TR,_S_KU,_S_LA,_S_A6,_S_AL,_S_A5,_S_VR,_S_PEZ,_S_VR9,_S_VR2,_S_BJ,_S_C8,_S_C2]))
F4=('<!--noart-->'
    +'<div class="fr-flow"><div class="fr-box" data-part="signal"><b>Mitohormesis.</b> Ristow and Zarse, 2010, use the name for a mitochondrial hormesis: reactive oxygen species inside mitochondria can induce an adaptive response. In their review, blocking that signal with antioxidants impairs the lifespan effect of glucose restriction and the health effect of exercise. They conclude that the species are required signals, not only damage. That is the review. The trial under it is narrower than a lifespan.</div>'
    +f'<p class="fr-arr">The human experiment is four weeks, not a lifetime</p>'
    +f'<div class="fr-box" data-part="exercise"><b>Exercise, blocked by the vitamins.</b> Ristow and colleagues, PNAS, 2009. Healthy young men. Vitamin C, {_Ri["vitaminCMg"]} mg a day, and vitamin E, {_Ri["vitaminEIu"]} IU a day. {_Ri["untrained"]} previously untrained, {_Ri["pretrained"]} pretrained. {_Ri["weeks"]} weeks of exercise. Insulin sensitivity rose only without the antioxidants, P &lt; 0.001 in both groups. The same split held for the induction of PPARγ, PGC-1α, PGC-1β, superoxide dismutases 1 and 2, and glutathione peroxidase. The abstract does not say how long anyone lived.</div></div>'
    +'<div class="fr-two" style="margin-top:10px"><div class="fr-col ok" data-part="agree"><h4>Broadly accepted</h4><ul>'
    +'<li>Reactive oxygen species are produced in normal aerobic metabolism, and oxidative damage to DNA rose with age in the human brain Mecocci measured and in the rodent tissues Hamilton and Van Remmen measured.</li>'
    +f'<li>Mitochondria-targeted catalase extended median and maximum lifespan in the Schriner mice, by {_Sc["medianMonths"]} months and {_Sc["maximumMonths"]} months on average. The abstract does not give the other localizations\' lifespans.</li>'
    +'<li>A proofreading failure in the mitochondrial polymerase caused a premature-aging phenotype in the Trifunovic mice and in the Kujoth mice.</li>'
    +f'<li>Mitochondrial dysfunction is one of the {_H["n2013"]} hallmarks in López-Otín and colleagues, 2013, and it remains one of the {_H["n2023"]} in 2023.</li>'
    +'<li>In the trials Bjelakovic and colleagues pooled, antioxidant supplements did not reduce mortality. In the low-bias trials they were associated with a small increase.</li></ul></div>'
    +'<div class="fr-col no"><h4>Contested, or a different reading</h4><ul data-part="against">'
    +'<li>Vaanholt and colleagues say the simple rate-of-living proportionality was rejected: birds have higher metabolic rates than mammals of the same size and live longer.</li>'
    +'<li>Sod2 heterozygotes had more oxidative DNA damage, more tumors, and the same mean and maximum lifespan. Only one of 18 manipulations in Pérez and colleagues, Sod1 deletion, shortened mouse life, and they leave open accelerated aging versus pathology.</li>'
    +'<li>Naked mole-rats combine a very long life with high oxidative damage and a glutathione peroxidase 70 times lower than the mouse.</li>'
    +'<li>Kujoth\'s mutator mice showed accelerated-aging features without increased oxidative-stress markers. Worms missing sod-2 lived longer. Worms missing all five superoxide dismutases had a normal lifespan.</li>'
    +'<li>The insulin-sensitivity benefit of exercise in Ristow\'s young men was blocked by vitamins C and E. Lambert\'s inverse association survived a sign test after phylogeny and missed significance on the regression.</li></ul>'
    +f'<p data-part="open"><b>Still open.</b> López-Otín and colleagues, 2013, say the mitochondrial free-radical theory needed re-evaluation: increased reactive oxygen species may prolong life in yeast and in C. elegans; in mice, raising mitochondrial reactive oxygen species did not accelerate aging; raising antioxidant defenses did not extend longevity; impairing mitochondria without raising reactive oxygen species did accelerate aging. When they state that theory, the reference they print is Harman, 1965, a paper on serum copper. It is not the 1972 article. They still list mitochondrial dysfunction as a hallmark, one of {_H["n2013"]} then and one of {_H["n2023"]} in 2023. What is open is how much of ordinary aging is that dysfunction, how much is the damage, and how much is a signal. The catalase result and the mutator mice are mice. The pills are not catalase targeted to human mitochondria. The worm results are worms.</p></div></div>'
    +fr_src([_S_RZ,_S_RI,_S_VA,_S_VR,_S_PEZ,_S_A6,_S_A5,_S_KU,_S_VR9,_S_VR2,_S_LA,_S_L3,_S_L23,_S_H65,_S_BJ]))
def fr_card(href,kicker,title,text):
    return f'<a href="../literature/{href}"><small>{kicker}</small><b>{fr_esc(title)}</b><span>{text}</span></a>'
F5=('<!--noart-->'
    +'<p data-part="bridge">These cards open pages in Peer-Reviewed Literature, part of this course. Each line is only what that page says.</p>'
    +'<div class="fr-cards" data-part="blood" style="margin-top:12px">'
    +fr_card('mtdna-mutations-blood-aging.html','Mitochondrial DNA','Copying errors in blood','In about 750,000 people, mitochondrial single-letter mutations in blood rose sharply at about age 60. The page says the pattern fitted copying errors, not oxidative damage. It limits that to blood, and says oxidative damage is not ruled out in aging generally.')
    +'</div><div class="fr-cards" data-part="fiber" style="margin-top:10px">'
    +fr_card('stress-adapted-fiber-state-mtdna-deletions.html','Mitochondrial DNA','Deleted copies in muscle','In mice missing Mpv17, deletions appeared by six months, and fibers that carried them held more mitochondrial DNA, together with raised Hsp60. The page says the abstract gives no animal or patient numbers, that the aging link is a suggestion, and that the human evidence is mitochondrial myopathy.')
    +'</div><div class="fr-cards" data-part="nad" style="margin-top:10px">'
    +fr_card('nad-replenishment-cardiomyocyte-senescence-aged-heart.html','Mitochondria and NAD','An aged male heart','Old male mice had lower heart NAD+, traced in part to limited mobilisation from mitochondria. Two months of an NAD precursor reduced heart-cell senescence. Old females did not respond. The page says the abstract gives no doses or animal numbers, and that this is mice and cells.')
    +'</div>')

FR_LESSON=[
 ('Where the ideas came from','Pearl\'s rate of living, Harman\'s free radicals, and the mitochondrial version.',F1),
 ('Where the radicals come from','A path from the respiratory chain to the damage. Not a measured rate.',F2),
 ('Predictions and the results','What the theories required, and what the named papers found.',F3),
 ('A signal, and what is settled','Mitohormesis, then what is broadly accepted and what is contested.',F4),
 ('From the literature','Reading-room pages on mitochondrial DNA and on NAD. Only what those pages say.',F5),
]
FR_CAPS=[
 'Pearl 1928 has no Crossref DOI. The inverse-proportionality sentence and the bird comparison are Vaanholt and colleagues, 2009. Harman 1956 has no Europe PMC abstract; the postulate is Pérez and colleagues, 2009, citing it. The metabolic-rate link is Harman, PNAS, 1981. Harman 1972 has no abstract; the description is Sastre, Pallardó, and Viña, 2000. Miquel\'s 1992 paper states the mitochondrial-DNA hypothesis and the 13 proteins. Sohal and Weindruch, 1996, state the oxidative stress hypothesis. The Drosophila support has no percentage in that abstract.',
 'The drawing is not to scale and has no leak fraction. Mecocci and colleagues, 1993: 10 people, ages 42 to 97. The 10-fold and 15-fold are mitochondrial DNA versus nuclear DNA. Hamilton and colleagues, 2001, still found an age-related rise after isolating DNA with sodium iodide. No fold-change is in that abstract.',
 'Schriner and colleagues, 2005: averages of 5 months median and 5.5 months maximum in the mitochondria-targeted mice. Trifunovic and colleagues, 2004: a three- to fivefold rise in point mutations. Kujoth and colleagues, 2005: no increase in oxidative-stress markers. Lambert and colleagues, 2007: the phylogeny-controlled regression fell short. Naked mole-rat numbers are Andziak and colleagues, 2005 and 2006. Sod2 is Van Remmen and colleagues, 2003. The 18 manipulations and the Sod1 caveat are Pérez and colleagues, 2009. Worm days are Van Raamsdonk and Hekimi, 2009, and they are double mutants. Supplements are Bjelakovic and colleagues, JAMA 2007, Cochrane 2008, and the Cochrane update of 2012. Vitamin C in the JAMA abstract has no printed relative risk.',
 'Mitohormesis is Ristow and Zarse, 2010. The trial is Ristow and colleagues, 2009: 1000 mg vitamin C, 400 IU vitamin E, 19 and 20 men, 4 weeks, insulin sensitivity, P < 0.001. Not a lifespan. The two columns separate what those papers support from what they dispute. López-Otín and colleagues, 2013 and 2023, keep mitochondrial dysfunction as a hallmark. Their 2013 citation for the mitochondrial free-radical theory is Harman 1965, on serum copper, not Harman 1972.',
 'Links open the existing reading-room pages. Nothing is added beyond what those pages say.',
]
FR_QUIZ=[
 ('What does this lesson use as the statement of Harman\'s 1956 theory?','Pérez and colleagues, 2009, citing that paper: oxygen free radicals formed in normal metabolism play a role in aging because oxidative damage to macromolecules increases. The 1956 paper has no abstract in Europe PMC (Figure 1).'),
 ('What is Pearl\'s rate-of-living theory, and what later rejected the simple version?','Life span and average mass-specific metabolic rate are inversely proportional, as Vaanholt and colleagues, 2009, state it. They say it was rejected because birds have higher metabolic rates than mammals of the same size and yet live longer. The 1928 book has no Crossref DOI (Figure 1).'),
 ('What did the 1972 paper add, and who is the source for that description?','Sastre, Pallardó, and Viña, 2000, say Harman suggested that mitochondria might be the biological clock, with oxygen consumption setting the rate of free-radical damage to mitochondria. The 1972 paper has no abstract here. The title is a question (Figure 1).'),
 ('How did Sohal and Weindruch move the comparison off metabolic rate?','They point to an inverse correlation between species longevity and the mitochondrial generation of superoxide and hydrogen peroxide, not to oxygen consumption itself. The Drosophila support in that abstract has no percentage (Figure 1).'),
 ('What did Schriner and colleagues measure, and what percentage is not used here?','Mitochondria-targeted catalase: average increases of 5 months in median lifespan and 5.5 months in maximum lifespan, with less damage. The abstract does not give the starting median, so 5 months is not converted to a percent (Figure 3).'),
 ('What did the mutator mice show, and what did Kujoth not find?','Trifunovic: a three- to fivefold rise in point mutations, more deletions, a shorter life, and a premature-aging phenotype. Kujoth: accelerated-aging features without increased markers of oxidative stress. Do not give Kujoth\'s oxidative result to Trifunovic (Figure 3).'),
 ('Why is Mecocci\'s 10-fold not an increase with age?','It is mitochondrial DNA versus nuclear DNA, in 10 people aged 42 to 97. The 15-fold is the same comparison in those older than 70. The abstract says the damage rose with age and does not give a fold for that rise (Figure 2).'),
 ('What did Van Remmen find in Sod2 heterozygotes?','Fifty percent manganese superoxide dismutase, more oxidative DNA damage, 100 percent more old mice with a tumor, and identical mean and maximum lifespan (Figure 3).'),
 ('What did the antioxidant-supplement analyses find, and which Cochrane year is wrong?','JAMA 2007: all trials together, relative risk 1.02, not significant; low-bias trials, 1.05, a small increase. The Cochrane review of that question is 2008, updated in 2012. It is not a 2007 Cochrane review. The 2012 authors found no evidence to support the supplements (Figure 3).'),
 ('What did Ristow\'s 2009 trial measure, and what did it not measure?','In healthy young men, four weeks of exercise raised insulin sensitivity only without vitamins C and E. It did not measure lifespan. Mitohormesis, as Ristow and Zarse name it, treats the reactive oxygen species as a signal (Figure 4).'),
 ('What do the reading-room pages not show?','They do not show that copying errors in blood, deleted mitochondrial DNA in a muscle disease, or an NAD precursor produced the historical rise in population life expectancy. The blood finding does not rule oxidative damage out of aging generally (Figure 5).')]
_figs=[figure_card(i,t,h,c,sub=s) for i,((t,s,h),c) in enumerate(zip(FR_LESSON,FR_CAPS),1)]
_quiz='<main class="lp-after"><section class="notes quiz" id="quiz"><h2>Quiz</h2><p class="lbl">Tap a question to see the answer</p>'+''.join(f'<details><summary>{n}. {q}</summary><p>{a}</p></details>' for n,(q,a) in enumerate(FR_QUIZ,1))+'</section></main>'
lesson_page('free-radical-mitochondria','Module 4 · Free Radical and Mitochondrial Theories · '+COURSE+' · '+UNIVERSITY_NAME,
 '<nav class="crumbs">'+CRUMB_HOME+' › Module 4</nav><h1>Module 4 · Free Radical and Mitochondrial Theories</h1>'
 '<p>Free radicals, the mitochondrial clock, and the experiments that kept or dropped each prediction. The history runs from Pearl and Harman to the oxidative stress hypothesis. The diagram is a path, not a rate. The rows under it are predictions against published results. Mitohormesis and the two columns separate a signal from the damage. The figure is on the left and the lesson script on the right. Harman 1956 and 1972 have no Europe PMC abstract; the wording for those two papers is taken from the later papers named beside them. Pearl 1928 has no Crossref DOI. Every other number is taken from the paper named beside it.</p>',
 _figs,_quiz+pn(('Module 3 · Telomeres and Replicative Senescence','telomere-senescence.html'),None))
